  "text": "@Injectable decorator in TypeScript is used to mark a class or a method as a service dependency. When used with a class, it tells the dependency injection (DI) system that the class itself needs to be resolved into an instance, typically through a factory or a provider that can instantiate the class.\n\nFor example, in the given code snippet:\n\n@Injectable\nclass FakeService { ... }\n\nThe FakeService class decorator itself should not be here. The actual service or component should have this decorator applied to the class or method where it needs to be resolved. If you're looking at a class that is being used as a service, and you see something like:\n\n@Injectable\nclass FakeService { ... }\n\nthis means that FakeService can be instantiated whenever the DI system tries to resolve it into an instance.\n\n@Injectable is a special decorator in Angular (or TypeScript with Angular) that you use to tell the Angular dependency injection system to create instances of the class as needed. It's not just a decorator applied to the class itself; it's mostly applied to the class or method that you can use to interact with services in your application, allowing Angular to manage the lifecycle and injection of services.\n\n@Injectable decorator with fully qualified names, almost never, on the applied class or component type, except for AOT (Ahead-of-Time) compilation. The compiler itself is responsible for detecting whether a decorator applied to a class should always be resolved AOT. The decorators apply to the compiler, not the code itself and do not make annotations available to parsing or materials validation.\n\n@Injectable is a decorator in TypeScript for the Angular dependency injection system.",
